## Using Entity Framework Core – A Step‑by‑Step Guide  

EF Core is a lightweight, extensible, cross‑platform ORM that lets you work with databases using strongly‑typed C# classes.  
Below is a “from‑scratch” walk‑through that covers:

| Step | What you’ll do | Key points |
|------|----------------|------------|
| 1 | Create a new project | .NET 6/7 console or ASP.NET Core Web API |
| 2 | Add EF Core packages | EF Core + provider (SQL Server, SQLite, PostgreSQL, etc.) |
| 3 | Define your entity classes | POCOs that map to tables |
| 4 | Create a `DbContext` | Holds `DbSet<T>`s and configuration |
| 5 | Configure the context | Connection string, logging, conventions |
| 6 | Scaffold migrations | Create/update database schema |
| 7 | Use the context | CRUD, LINQ queries, async/await |
| 8 | (Optional) Seed data | Initial data via `OnModelCreating` or a separate seeder |
| 9 | (Optional) Use repositories / unit‑of‑work | Patterns for larger apps |

---

### 1. Create a Project

```bash
dotnet new console -n BlogSample
cd BlogSample
```

*(If you’re building a web API, replace `console` with `webapi`.)*

---

### 2. Add EF Core Packages

```bash
# Core + provider
dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore
dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite     # <-- choose your provider
dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Tools     # for design‑time tools
```

> **Tip:** For SQL Server, replace `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite` with  
> `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer`.  
> For PostgreSQL, use `Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL`.

---

### 3. Define Your Entities

```csharp
// Models/Blog.cs
public class Blog
{
    public int Id { get; set; }           // PK
    public string Url { get; set; } = null!;
    public ICollection<Post> Posts { get; } = new List<Post>();
}

// Models/Post.cs
public class Post
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Title { get; set; } = null!;
    public string Content { get; set; } = null!;

    public int BlogId { get; set; }
    public Blog Blog { get; set; } = null!;
}
```

> **Why POCO?**  
> Plain‑Old‑CLR‑Object → no dependencies on EF internals, easy to unit‑test.

---

### 4. Create the `DbContext`

```csharp
// Data/BlogContext.cs
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

public class BlogContext : DbContext
{
    public DbSet<Blog> Blogs => Set<Blog>();
    public DbSet<Post> Posts => Set<Post>();

    public BlogContext(DbContextOptions<BlogContext> options)
        : base(options)
    {
    }

    protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
    {
        // Optional fluent API
        modelBuilder.Entity<Blog>()
            .HasMany(b => b.Posts)
            .WithOne(p => p.Blog)
            .HasForeignKey(p => p.BlogId);

        // Seed data
        modelBuilder.Entity<Blog>().HasData(
            new Blog { Id = 1, Url = "https://dotnet.microsoft.com/" }
        );
    }
}
```

---

### 5. Configure the Context

#### Console app

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var services = new ServiceCollection();
services.AddDbContext<BlogContext>(options =>
    options.UseSqlite("Data Source=blog.db")
           .EnableSensitiveDataLogging()   // only in dev
           .UseLoggerFactory(LoggerFactory.Create(builder => builder.AddConsole()))
);

var provider = services.BuildServiceProvider();
using var scope = provider.CreateScope();
var context = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<BlogContext>();

// Ensure database is created (runs pending migrations)
context.Database.Migrate();
```

#### ASP.NET Core (minimal API)

Add in `Program.cs`:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddDbContext<BlogContext>(options =>
    options.UseSqlite(builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("Default")));

builder.Services.AddControllers();
builder.Services.AddEndpointsApiExplorer();
builder.Services.AddSwaggerGen();
```

In `appsettings.json`:

```json
{
  "ConnectionStrings": {
    "Default": "Data Source=blog.db"
  }
}
```

---

### 6. Scaffold Migrations

```bash
dotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate
dotnet ef database update
```

> **What happens?**  
> - `InitialCreate` generates a migration class with `Up` / `Down`.  
> - `database update` applies the migration (creates tables).

You can check the generated migration file in `Migrations/`.  
Run `dotnet ef migrations list` to see all migrations.

---

### 7. Use the Context – CRUD & Queries

```csharp
// Example: add a blog and a post
var blog = new Blog { Url = "https://example.com" };
var post = new Post { Title = "First Post", Content = "Hello World!", Blog = blog };

context.Blogs.Add(blog);     // EF tracks the whole graph
await context.SaveChangesAsync();
```

**Read**

```csharp
var blogs = await context.Blogs
    .Include(b => b.Posts)          // eager load
    .Where(b => b.Url.Contains("dotnet"))
    .OrderBy(b => b.Id)
    .ToListAsync();

var post = await context.Posts
    .FirstOrDefaultAsync(p => p.Title == "First Post");
```

**Update**

```csharp
post.Title = "Updated Title";
await context.SaveChangesAsync();
```

**Delete**

```csharp
context.Posts.Remove(post);   // or context.Posts.RemoveRange(posts);
await context.SaveChangesAsync();
```

#### Working with `DbSet` directly

```csharp
// Add range
await context.Posts.AddRangeAsync(new List<Post>{ ... });
await context.SaveChangesAsync();

// Raw SQL
var count = await context.Blogs
    .FromSqlRaw("SELECT * FROM Blogs WHERE Id = {0}", 1)
    .CountAsync();
```

---

### 8. Seeding Data (advanced)

You can seed in `OnModelCreating` (shown above) or create a separate **seed** method that runs once after migrations.

```csharp
public static class DbSeeder
{
    public static async Task SeedAsync(BlogContext context)
    {
        if (!await context.Blogs.AnyAsync())
        {
            context.Blogs.AddRange(new[]
            {
                new Blog { Url = "https://blog1.com" },
                new Blog { Url = "https://blog2.com" }
            });
            await context.SaveChangesAsync();
        }
    }
}
```

Call it from `Main` after `context.Database.Migrate()`.

---

### 9. (Optional) Repositories & Unit‑of‑Work

For larger projects, you might wrap data access in a repository:

```csharp
public interface IGenericRepository<T> where T : class
{
    Task<T?> GetByIdAsync(int id);
    Task<IEnumerable<T>> ListAsync();
    Task AddAsync(T entity);
    void Remove(T entity);
    Task<int> SaveChangesAsync();
}

public class GenericRepository<T> : IGenericRepository<T> where T : class
{
    private readonly BlogContext _ctx;
    public GenericRepository(BlogContext ctx) => _ctx = ctx;

    public async Task<T?> GetByIdAsync(int id) =>
        await _ctx.Set<T>().FindAsync(id);

    public async Task<IEnumerable<T>> ListAsync() =>
        await _ctx.Set<T>().ToListAsync();

    public async Task AddAsync(T entity) => await _ctx.Set<T>().AddAsync(entity);
    public void Remove(T entity) => _ctx.Set<T>().Remove(entity);
    public Task<int> SaveChangesAsync() => _ctx.SaveChangesAsync();
}
```

Register in DI:

```csharp
services.AddScoped(typeof(IGenericRepository<>), typeof(GenericRepository<>));
```

---

## Quick Reference – Commands & CLI

| Task | Command |
|------|---------|
| Add EF Core tooling | `dotnet tool install --global dotnet-ef` |
| Scaffold a migration | `dotnet ef migrations add MigrationName` |
| Apply migrations | `dotnet ef database update` |
| Scaffold database (reverse‑engineer) | `dotnet ef dbcontext scaffold "ConnString" Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite -o Models` |
| Run the app | `dotnet run` |
| Package reference | `dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer` |

---

## Common Gotchas & Tips

| Problem | Fix |
|---------|-----|
| “Unable to connect to the database” | Verify the connection string. For SQLite, path must be correct relative to working directory. |
| “Missing migrations” | Run `dotnet ef migrations list` to confirm. If none, create one. |
| “Entity Framework Core doesn't support Lazy Loading by default” | Install `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Proxies` and enable `UseLazyLoadingProxies()` in `OnConfiguring`. |
| “Performance slow when loading related data” | Use `.Include()` or projection (`Select`) to fetch only needed columns. |
| “SQL Injection risk with raw SQL” | Always use parameter placeholders (`{0}`, `{1}`) or `FromSqlInterpolated`. |
| “Database context not registered” | In ASP.NET Core, remember to add `services.AddDbContext<YourContext>()`. |
| “Concurrent update exception” | Use `DbUpdateConcurrencyException` handling or `rowversion` columns. |

---

## TL;DR

1. **Add EF Core packages** for your provider.  
2. **Define POCO entities** and a `DbContext`.  
3. **Configure the context** (connection string, logging).  
4. **Run `dotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate`** and `dotnet ef database update`.  
5. **Inject the context** (DI or service provider).  
6. **Use `DbSet<T>`** for CRUD; LINQ for queries; `await context.SaveChangesAsync()` to persist.  
7. **Optional**: seed data, use repositories, enable lazy loading, or generate migrations programmatically.

With this workflow you can evolve your data model, run migrations, and perform CRUD operations seamlessly in any .NET 6/7 or later project. Happy coding!
